<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>@PROJECTNAME@ — Complete User Guide</title>
<style>
body{font-family:"Segoe UI",Arial,sans-serif;background:#fff;color:#1f2430;margin:24px;line-height:1.45}
h1{color:#1f66e6;border-bottom:2px solid #d6ddea;padding-bottom:6px}
h2{color:#14479e;margin-top:26px}
h3{color:#14479e;margin-top:18px}
table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;margin:12px 0}th,td{border:1px solid #d6ddea;padding:7px;text-align:left}
th{background:#1f66e6;color:#fff}.warn{background:#fff6e8;border:1px solid #f0c987;padding:12px}
code{background:#f2f3f5;padding:1px 4px}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<h1>ECU MEMS Manager — User Guide</h1>
<p><b>Version @APP_VERSION@</b> — Diagnostic software for Rover MEMS ECUs.</p>

<div class="warn"><b>Safety:</b> actuator tests can operate pumps, fans, valves, injectors and ignition components. Perform tests only when the vehicle is secured and you understand the consequences of the selected command.</div>

<h2>1. Connection</h2>
<p>Connect the supported diagnostic interface to the vehicle and select the correct serial port in <b>Options → Edit settings</b>. Switch the ignition on, then press <b>Connect</b>. The communication indicators show the current connection state. Use <b>Disconnect</b> before intentionally ending a session.</p>
<p>If the vehicle ignition is switched off and communication is lost, the software leaves the polling loop cleanly and can reconnect when communication becomes available again.</p>

<h2>2. Overview tab</h2>
<p>The Overview tab is the default tab shown at startup. It provides the main live gauges and status indicators for quick diagnosis: engine speed, manifold pressure, coolant and intake temperatures, battery voltage, throttle information, lambda information, idle control and ignition values.</p>
<p>In the light theme the gauges use the original light appearance. In the dark theme the gauge faces are dark, with light needles, graduations and values. Red warning areas remain red and green nominal areas remain green.</p>

<h2>3. Settings tab</h2>
<p>The Settings tab contains service adjustments such as idle, fuel and ignition-related corrections. Tooltips explain the purpose and risks of each adjustment. The hot-idle correction used for decoding field 7D14-15 follows the value actually configured here; it is not a hard-coded constant.</p>

<h2>4. Errors tab</h2>
<p>This tab displays ECU fault information, recorded anomalies and status checks. Faults should be read and understood before they are cleared. Some counters or status fields may require a complete ECU reset before they return to their default state.</p>

<h2>5. Actuators tab</h2>
<p>Use this tab to test supported actuators. Available functions depend on the ECU and protocol state. Do not run injector, ignition or heater tests unless the vehicle is in a safe condition.</p>

<h2>6. All measurements</h2>
<p>The <b>All measurements</b> tab shows each parameter in four columns:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Help</th><th>ECU received value</th><th>Interpreted value</th></tr>
<tr><td>Example: battery voltage</td><td>Tooltip</td><td>138</td><td>13.8 V</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The received value is the raw value returned by the ECU. The interpreted value applies the same decoding rules used elsewhere in the program. Undocumented fields remain visible without inventing a conversion.</p>

<h2>7. All data</h2>
<p>This historical/raw-data view exposes the detailed MEMS fields and their decoded values. It is useful when comparing protocol bytes with interpreted measurements.</p>

<h2>8. ECU / ROSCO</h2>
<p>The ECU / ROSCO tab exposes documented session and read commands such as D0, D1, D2, F0 and session commands F2–F5. TX/RX data is shown in hexadecimal. D3 ECU recoding remains intentionally disabled.</p>

<h2>9. Automatic diagnostics</h2>
<p>The automatic diagnostic panel evaluates current ECU values and reports normal values, warnings and anomalies. It can capture a reference and export a report. Coil charge time is checked against the expected range used by this application.</p>

<h2>10. Log analysis</h2>
<p>The Analysis tab can read supported CSV/TXT diagnostic logs, display selected channels and overlay curves. Use the channel list to choose which measurements are displayed.</p>

<h2>11. Snapshots</h2>
<p><b>Snapshot</b> saves an image of the current program window in the <code>captures</code> folder next to the executable. <b>View captures</b> opens the integrated capture viewer. From there you can preview, open/enlarge or delete a selected capture. Deletion requires confirmation.</p>

<h2>12. Tooltips and help bubble</h2>
<p>Wherever a hover comment is available, ECU MEMS Manager uses the same embedded speech-bubble icon with three dots. The icon is stored inside the program resources so its appearance does not depend on the Windows version or computer.</p>

<h2>13. Options</h2>
<p>Options include the serial device, temperature units, interface theme, language and Desktop-shortcut setting. French and English are supported. Changing language restarts the application so all translated UI elements are recreated consistently.</p>
<p>When <b>Create and maintain a Desktop shortcut</b> is enabled, the shortcut is created immediately after confirmation. At later startups the program checks that it still exists and recreates it if necessary. The shortcut uses the KEY icon shipped with the application.</p>

<h2>14. Themes</h2>
<p><b>Light:</b> original light gauge faces and light interface.</p>
<p><b>Dark:</b> dark interface and dark gauge faces with high-contrast light text, graduations and needles. Table alternate-row colors are also dark to keep all text readable.</p>

<h2>15. Language</h2>
<p>French and English translations cover tab titles, buttons, labels, tooltips, dialogs, diagnostic messages, capture controls and dynamically generated interface text. The selected language is stored in the application settings.</p>

<h2>16. Supported ECU families</h2>
<p>The project includes support paths for Rover MEMS 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6. Actual features available in a session depend on the ECU and vehicle configuration.</p>

<h2>17. Basic diagnostic guidance</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Checks</th></tr>
<tr><td>No communication</td><td>Ignition, correct serial port, diagnostic cable, ECU power and grounds.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abnormal MAP value</td><td>Vacuum hose, manifold connection and internal MAP measurement.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unstable idle</td><td>Idle switch, IAC position, air leaks, coolant temperature and fuel trims.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Lambda fault</td><td>Sensor warm-up, wiring, voltage activity and closed-loop state.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Abnormal coil charge time</td><td>Battery voltage, primary circuit, coil and wiring.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>18. Closing the application</h2>
<p>The application stops automatic reconnect activity during shutdown so a lost ECU connection cannot prevent the window from closing normally.</p>
</body>
</html>
